WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.000
Critical thinking depends on information quality.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A source may sound polished, confident, or popular and still be weak.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Source evaluation asks who produced the information, what expertise they have,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.000
what evidence they provide, what incentives may influence them,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.000
and what uncertainty remains.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
The point is not to distrust everything.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.000
It is to assign confidence in proportion to support.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.190
Credibility is not only about status.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.000
Expertise should match the topic.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
A person may be highly qualified in one area and unreliable in another.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.310
Incentives matter because sources may be selling, defending, simplifying, persuading,

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:30.995
or trying to win attention.

00:00:30.995 --> 00:00:35.050
Conflicts of interest do not automatically make information false,

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.975
but they should shape how carefully the claim is checked.

00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:41.265
Primary sources are closer to the original evidence, data, event, or official statement.

00:00:41.265 --> 00:00:45.000
Secondary sources interpret, summarize, or comment on primary material.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:45.995
Both can be useful.

00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:48.220
Primary sources reduce distance from the evidence,

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.190
while secondary sources may provide context or explanation.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:54.610
Critical thinkers know which kind they are using and avoid treating interpretation as if

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:56.185
it were the original record.

00:00:56.185 --> 00:00:58.000
Data quality matters.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Ask how the data was collected, what the sample represents, what might be missing,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.970
and whether the measure actually supports the claim.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.090
At a high level, sample size affects reliability,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.995
but size alone does not fix biased collection or poor measurement.

00:01:08.995 --> 00:01:13.370
Cherry-picking occurs when only convenient evidence is shown while conflicting evidence is

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:13.995
ignored.

00:01:13.995 --> 00:01:16.000
AI-generated material deserves the same discipline.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.485
Fluent output can be useful for drafting, summarizing, brainstorming,

00:01:19.485 --> 00:01:22.929
or comparing possibilities, but fluency is not truth.

00:01:22.929 --> 00:01:27.000
AI output can be incomplete, unsupported, outdated, or wrong.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Treat it as material to examine, not as authority.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Verify important claims against appropriate sources before relying on them.
